WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.400
Quick question. What number defines you? Is
it your age? Those lotto digits you just know

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.380
will pay off someday?

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.250
Maybe you think there's no number that defines
you. But corporate America disagrees. Industry

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:20.070
defines you with three digits, your credit
score, and it influences everything from how

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:24.650
much you'll pay to borrow money to whether
you can buy a cellphone, land a job, or rent

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:28.810
an apartment.
It's not easy to make sense of your finances.

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:32.680
I'm Shahien Nasiripour and I want to meet
people with an obsession to beat the system

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.460
and never leave money on the table.

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.160
 

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:46.370
It's become such a popular measure of character
that there's a dating service that matches

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:52.870
people off of it. Maybe it's not for you.
It's definitely not for me. Around 200 million

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:59.690
Americans have FICO scores, but only about
1.5% of them have that perfect 850 number.

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:03.879
One of the folks who knows how to join the
850 club is Stan Kellman. He learned to game

00:01:03.879 --> 00:01:09.260
a system after his score fell below 600 during
the Great Recession. Let's give him a ring

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:16.070
and find out how he did it.
Hey, Stan, thanks so much for taking the time

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.030
to chat.
Thank you for having me.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.350
What moved you to want to achieve an 850 credit
score?

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:29.800
I just happened to have the misfortune of
graduating with my MBA in the recession and

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:40.130
got into some debt. In the process of settling
that debt, my credit score dropped. What it's

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:47.590
like to live with a low score, it really sucks.
If you have a mark on your credit report,

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:51.210
it will not go away for seven years, for 10
if you have a bankruptcy.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:56.320
You mentioned that you had negotiated with
your creditors. How did you go about doing

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.790
that?
You'll get daily collection calls and they'll

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:05.180
want you to make a payment. What you don't
realize is once you make a payment, your clock

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:12.689
gets reset. By law, after 180 days of nonpayment
is when they are required to write it off

00:02:12.689 --> 00:02:18.209
for accounting purposes, and right before
the 180 day mark is when you can call them

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:22.459
and make a deal.
I could settle some cards for as low as 20

00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:26.129
cents on the dollar. You are not paying back
what you promised to pay back, but at the

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:32.540
same time, there are big banks that got billions
of dollars from the government in bailout

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:35.900
funds and I didn't. So that was my own
personal bailout.

00:02:35.909 --> 00:02:45.159
Why didn't you just stop when you got to 780?
Why did you try to get that elusive 850?

00:02:45.159 --> 00:02:49.290
Because that's my personality. I wanted my
perfect score. The scores are not developed

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:55.359
for you. They're developed for the banks to
evaluate your credit worthness. They're not

00:02:55.359 --> 00:03:01.599
a measure of your personal financial health.
You can be deep in debt and have a perfect

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:06.359
score, and that's not good for you. And it's
better to have a middling score, but have no

00:03:06.359 --> 00:03:08.790
debt.
Let's look at how these credit scores are

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:14.769
weighted. Peoples with perfect 850 scores
never make late payments. The 850ers use maybe

00:03:14.769 --> 00:03:19.510
5% of the big credit limit and have had a
diverse mix of loans and card accounts over

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:23.859
the years. They also haven't applied for any
new credit recently.

00:03:23.859 --> 00:03:27.159
It's not the fairest system, but you really
don't need to be perfect to get it to work

00:03:27.159 --> 00:03:32.779
for you. Wisebread.com founder, Janet Alvarez,
raised her score 300 points, and she's got

00:03:32.779 --> 00:03:37.230
tips on how to raise your score and keep it
there.

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:43.400
In order to get the perfect 850 or 860 credit
score, it's going to take sort of impeccable

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:46.669
performance on every aspect of your credit.
But you don't really need it. You don't need

00:03:46.669 --> 00:03:49.889
a perfect score to get the best rates or the
best offers. You just need a really good score.

00:03:49.889 --> 00:03:54.099
Younger people are inadvertently penalized
because if you're younger, you're just not

00:03:54.099 --> 00:03:57.700
going to have a lot of credit history and
to get the maximum boost out of this component

00:03:57.700 --> 00:04:00.760
of your credit score, you're going to want
a credit history of 30 years or longer. Even

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.419
if your credit isn't great, if you do qualify
for more credit, ask for higher credit limits

00:04:05.419 --> 00:04:10.040
on your existing cards or try to get new cards.
Again, that improves your credit utilization

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:14.610
ratio so that opens your score. It can boost
it by something like 50 to 100 points within

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:17.380
a month, depending on how much additional
credit you take on.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.860
There's a different score generated for the
type of loan or the type of credit you're

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:26.000
seeking, so for the purposes of credit cards
or a car loan or a home loan, you might get

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.450
different scores, and that's just because
they're looking at different selection criteria

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.530
based on what you're borrowing for.
So get to work on your score. Give yourself

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:35.720
some credit along the way.

